# Solution — GIAM 1.3 Problem 2

## Problem

The sentence "Every metallic element is a solid at room temperature." is false. Why?

## The Short Answer

The sentence is **universally quantified** — it claims something about *every* metallic element. A universal statement is false as soon as there is a *single* exception, and there is one:

$$$
\boxed{\;\text{Mercury (Hg) is a metallic element that is } \textit{liquid} \text{ at room temperature.}\;}
$$$

Mercury melts at about $ -38.8\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} $, far below room temperature ($ \approx 25\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} $), so at room temperature it is a *liquid*, not a solid. That one counterexample makes the statement false. $ \blacksquare $

## Why One Counterexample Suffices

The sentence has the logical form of a universally quantified statement — in fact a *universal conditional*:

$$$
\forall x \,\bigl( \mathrm{Metal}(x) \rightarrow \mathrm{SolidAtRT}(x) \bigr),
$$$

"for every element $ x $, if $ x $ is a metal then $ x $ is solid at room temperature." To *disprove* a "$ \forall $" statement, we use the **negation rule** for quantifiers:

$$$
\neg\, \forall x\, P(x) \;\;\equiv\;\; \exists x \,\neg P(x).
$$$

So the negation of our sentence is the *existential* statement

$$$
\exists x \,\bigl( \mathrm{Metal}(x) \,\wedge\, \neg\,\mathrm{SolidAtRT}(x) \bigr),
$$$

"there exists a metal that is *not* solid at room temperature." To establish this existential, we need just **one** witness — and mercury is it. A single counterexample is logically complete: it does not merely cast doubt on the universal claim, it *refutes* it outright.

## The Counterexample, Visually

!(100%)[Common metals at room temperature: all solid except mercury](problem-2-metals.svg "Iron, gold, lead, sodium, and gallium are all solid at room temperature; mercury alone is liquid (melting point −39 °C). The single liquid metal is the counterexample that falsifies the universal claim.")

## Remark — Disproving "All" vs Proving "All": An Asymmetry

There is a deep asymmetry between proving and disproving a universal statement, and this problem showcases the easy half:

- To **prove** $ \forall x\, P(x) $ — that *every* metal is solid — you would have to verify it for *all* metals (infinitely many cases in general; here, every metallic element). No finite check suffices on its own; you need a general argument.
- To **disprove** $ \forall x\, P(x) $ — to show it is false — you need only *one* counterexample.

This is why universal statements are often "easy to kill, hard to confirm." We saw the same asymmetry in Chapter 1.2: the conjectures "$ 2^p - 1 $ is always prime" (Problem 5) and "$ x^2 - 31x + 257 $ is always prime" (Problem 6) were each demolished by a single counterexample, while *proving* a universal prime-generating claim is generally hopeless. The mercury example is the chemistry-flavoured version of exactly that move.

## Remark — Mercury Is the *Only* Liquid Metallic Element at Room Temperature

Mercury is special: among the metallic elements, it is the *unique* one that is liquid at standard room temperature. (The only other element that is liquid at room temperature is **bromine** — but bromine is a *nonmetal* (a halogen), so it is not a counterexample to *this* sentence, which is about metals.) Mercury's liquidity is itself a striking physical fact, traceable to relativistic effects on its electrons that weaken the metallic bonding — which is why a single metal stands out as the exception.

## Remark — The Definition of "Room Temperature" Matters

The sentence is false under *any* reasonable reading, but it is worth noting how close several other metals come — which is exactly why precise definitions (the recurring theme of these sections) matter:

| Metal       | Melting point | Solid at $ 25\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} $? |
| :---        | :---:         | :---:                                 |
| Mercury (Hg)| $ -39\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} $   | **no — liquid** |
| Caesium (Cs)| $ 28\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} $    | yes, but barely |
| Gallium (Ga)| $ 30\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} $    | yes, but melts in your hand |
| Rubidium (Rb)| $ 39\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} $   | yes |
| Sodium (Na) | $ 98\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} $    | yes |

Caesium and gallium melt only a few degrees *above* a typical room, and **gallium famously melts in the warmth of a held hand** ($ 30\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} $). If "room temperature" were taken to be a warm $ 30\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} $ day, caesium and gallium would *also* become counterexamples. But mercury is a counterexample regardless: at no ordinary room temperature is it solid. So the sentence fails robustly — mercury alone settles it, and the borderline metals only pile on.

## Remark — How to State a *True* Version

It is instructive to ask what nearby statement *is* true. Several repairs work:

- **Weaken the quantifier** to existential: "*Some* metallic element is a solid at room temperature." — true (most of them are).
- **Restrict the universe**: "Every metallic element *other than mercury* is a solid at room temperature." — true under a $ 25\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C} $ reading (and very nearly true in general).
- **Change the predicate**: "Every metallic element is a solid *or a liquid* at room temperature." — true (no metal is gaseous at room temperature).

Each repair illustrates a standard logical move: when a universal claim is refuted by counterexamples, you either shrink the domain, weaken the quantifier, or loosen the predicate until the survivors all comply. Knowing *which* repair you want is the difference between a sloppy claim and a precise theorem — the discipline this section is training.
